KNOWNBUG
main.cpp

^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ cross cast, one call: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ downcast, one call: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ to void \*, one call: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ reference cross cast, one call: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
The run-time check of a dynamic_cast reads its operand more than once and
was skipped for an operand with side effects: a cross cast then
reinterpreted the pointer (offset 0 instead of the NE subobject's), and the
reference form was rejected ("type mismatch on dynamic_cast").  Such an
operand is now evaluated once into a local by a statement expression.
Without a declaration of std::bad_cast the reference form is still
rejected.
